---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Cmd from '../components/Cmd.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import { faqPick, site } from '../content/site';

const title = 'Codex CLI memory across sessions | hippo-memory';
const description =
  'Codex CLI memory across sessions: hippo adds a block to AGENTS.md and keeps lessons in a local store you can correct, shared with Claude Code and Cursor.';

// The Codex entry of the hook templates in src/cli.ts, up to its closing capture note.
const fence = '```';
const block = [
  '## Project Memory (Hippo)',
  '',
  'At the start of every task, run:',
  `${fence}bash`,
  'hippo context --auto --budget 1500',
  fence,
  'Read the output before writing any code.',
  '',
  'On errors or unexpected behaviour:',
  `${fence}bash`,
  'hippo remember "<description of what went wrong>" --error',
  fence,
  '',
  'On task completion:',
  `${fence}bash`,
  'hippo outcome --good',
  fence,
];
const codexMemories = 'https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/customization/memories';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Codex" published="2026-09-28">
  <Nav />
  <main id="main">
    <PageIntro eyebrow="Codex CLI">
      <Fragment slot="title">Codex CLI memory <span class="text-gradient">across sessions</span></Fragment>
      <p>
        Codex reads <code>AGENTS.md</code> files before it starts work. <code>hippo init</code> adds a short block there
        telling Codex to run <code>hippo context --auto</code> at the start of every task, so lessons from earlier
        sessions, and from your other agents, come back when they match the task.
      </p>
    </PageIntro>

    <Section title="Set it up">
      <Cmd lines={[site.installCmd, 'hippo init', '# optional: capture Codex sessions too', 'hippo hook install codex']} />
      <p>
        Capturing Codex sessions is opt-in. <code>hippo hook install codex</code> wraps the Codex launcher,
        <code>hippo hook uninstall codex</code> removes the wrapper, and <code>hippo init</code> prints the command when it
        detects Codex. <code>hippo doctor</code> checks the install.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="What hippo adds to AGENTS.md" alt>
      <Cmd lines={block} prompt={false} />
      <p>
        It closes with a note that session-end capture runs by itself once the Codex wrapper is installed, and a
        <code>hippo capture --stdin</code> command for a manual summary when it is not. The block sits between
        <code>&lt;!-- hippo:start --&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;!-- hippo:end --&gt;</code> markers, so the rest of your
        <code>AGENTS.md</code> stays yours.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Codex memories and hippo">
      <p>
        Codex has a Memories feature of its own: it can turn useful context from eligible prior chats into local memory
        files under <code>~/.codex/memories/</code>. OpenAI's docs say to keep required team guidance in
        <code>AGENTS.md</code> or checked-in documentation, and to treat memories as a helpful recall layer, not as the only
        source for rules that must always apply
        (<a class="link" href={codexMemories} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenAI docs<span class="sr-only"> (opens in new tab)</span></a>,
        checked 2026-09-28).
      </p>
      <p>
        Hippo is a recall layer too, with two differences. You can mark a lesson wrong with <code>hippo outcome --bad</code>
        or replace one with <code>hippo supersede</code>. And the store sits in your project, with markdown mirrors you can
        commit, shared with Claude Code, Cursor and any MCP client.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Faq
      items={faqPick(['How do I give Codex memory across sessions?', 'Which agents does hippo work with?', 'What happens when a memory turns out to be wrong?'])}
      heading="Codex questions"
      moreHref="/faq/"
    />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
